//? Title: Gram–Schmidt: subtract overlap, measure what remains
//? Subtitle: Actual modified projections, absolute thresholds and finite-precision orthogonality
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given overlapping directions, we want unit directions whose coordinates can be
read through dot products. Exact arithmetic permits a basis with the same span,
but rounding and small residuals complicate this construction. Modified
Gram–Schmidt subtracts each projection from the current residual. This report
follows actual native projection states for the original two-dimensional
example, derives the exact invariants and operation counts, and measures
threshold and conditioning effects. A separately selected second pass
illustrates reorthogonalization. Finally, four centered Iris measurement columns
provide an order-dependent orthogonal representation of the observations.
)

\toc

> Begin with the original two vectors

Let \(v_1=(2,1)\) and \(v_2=(1,3)\). In exact arithmetic,

$$q_1=\frac{(2,1)}{\sqrt5},\quad
\alpha=v_2\cdot q_1=\sqrt5,\quad
u=v_2-\alpha q_1=(-1,2),\quad q_2=\frac{(-1,2)}{\sqrt5}.$$
\alttext(Normalize the first vector. The second vector has overlap square root five with it; subtracting that component leaves minus one comma two, which is then normalized.)

Here the projection of \(v_2\) coincides with \(v_1\). The diagram draws that
shared segment once in gold. The green translated segment shows the remainder,
and the two short unit arrows show the accepted directions. The table prints the
actual floating-point coefficient and states, without claiming their rounded dot
product is exactly zero.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gram_projection.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto t = dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt_trace({{2, 1}, {1, 3}});
    const auto &p = t.projections.at(0);
    const auto &q = t.run.basis;
    std::print("| accepted row | x | y |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < q.size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {:.17g} | {:.17g} |\n", i, q[i][0], q[i][1]);
    std::print("\nActual coefficient {:.17g}; residual ({:.17g}, {:.17g}); residual norm {:.17g}.\n",
               p.coefficient, p.after[0], p.after[1], t.rows[1].norm);
    dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions format;
    format.scientific = {"max_gram_error", "relative_projection_residual"};
    format.digits = 8;
    std::print("\n{}\n",
               dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt_quality({{2, 1}, {1, 3}}, q).to_md(
               format));
    const double px = p.coefficient * q[0][0], py = p.coefficient * q[0][1];
    dm::graphics::Diagram d(130, 1000);
    dm::graphics::DiagramStroke gray;
    gray.arrow = true;
    gray.width = 2.5;
    gray.color = {100, 116, 139};
    auto gold = gray;
    gold.color = {202, 138, 4};
    auto green = gray;
    green.color = {5, 150, 105};
    auto blue = gray;
    blue.color = {37, 99, 235};
    auto red = gray;
    red.color = {220, 38, 38};
    d.path({{0, 0}, {p.before[0], p.before[1]}}, gray).path({{0, 0}, {px, py}},
                                                            gold).path(
        {{px, py}, {p.before[0], p.before[1]}}, green).path(
        {{0, 0}, {q[0][0], q[0][1]}}, blue).path({{0, 0}, {q[1][0], q[1][1]}},
                                                 red).text({1, 3.4},
                                                           "v2 = (1,3)", 23).text({2.5, .9}, "v1 = projection", 23).text({2.3, 2.3}, "remainder (-1,2)", 22).text({1.05, .05}, "q1", 22).text({-.9, 1}, "q2", 22).text({.8, 4.1}, "Actual projection and accepted unit directions", 25).save_svg("build/gram_projection.svg");
    if (q.size() != 2 || p.input_row != 1 || p.basis_row != 0)
        throw std::runtime_error("original pair");
}
