\caption(Projection and residual states are captured inside the shared native kernel. The equal-scale diagram represents their geometry; small rounding differences are retained in the printed table and diagnostics.)
\alttext(A grey second input vector decomposes into a gold projection along the first vector and a green perpendicular remainder. Blue and red unit vectors show the resulting approximate orthonormal directions.)

> Derive the exact process, then identify the computed one

For input vector \(v_j\), start with \(u^{(0)}=v_j\). Modified Gram–Schmidt uses
the current residual, rather than the unchanged original vector, for each
coefficient:

$$\alpha_i=q_i^T u^{(i-1)},\qquad
u^{(i)}=u^{(i-1)}-\alpha_iq_i,\qquad
q_j=u^{(r)}/\|u^{(r)}\|_2.$$
\alttext(At each step, take the dot product with the current residual, subtract that component, and normalize the final nonzero residual.)

If previous directions satisfy \(q_i^Tq_l=\delta_{il}\), subtraction makes
\(q_i^Tu^{(i)}=0\), while preserving zero overlap with earlier directions.
Induction proves orthogonality in exact arithmetic. Each subtraction changes a
vector only by an existing basis combination; adding a nonzero residual
therefore preserves the processed span. An exactly zero residual signals exact
dependence in this model.

The implementation uses sequential double dot products and
\(\sqrt{\sum u_i^2}\), then accepts only a computed norm strictly above an
absolute tolerance, default \(10^{-12}\). Its ordinary API uses one projection
pass. Finite-precision residuals need not be exactly orthogonal, and threshold
rejection is not a proof of exact dependence. Rescaling or reordering inputs can
change the accepted count. Even a dependent row can leave rounding noise,
especially with a zero or overly small threshold.

The new run/trace APIs can select two full modified projection passes before
normalization. The second pass removes overlap left by the first against the
same accepted directions. It costs extra dots and updates; it is not a
rank-revealing factorization or an unconditional orthogonality guarantee. No
pivoting, relative threshold or scaled norm is silently substituted for the
original loop.

> Separate absolute scale from conditioning

Two independent coordinate axes with norm below the default threshold are both
discarded. The represented subspace is still two-dimensional mathematically. For
an ill-conditioned example, use the three independent rows
\((1,\varepsilon,0,0)\), \((1,0,\varepsilon,0)\), \((1,0,0,\varepsilon)\). Their
directions become nearly parallel as \(\varepsilon\) shrinks. The following
table reports accepted counts beside the maximum Gram defect,

$$E_Q=\max_{i,l}|q_i^Tq_l-\delta_{il}|.$$
\alttext(The maximum Gram defect is the largest deviation of the computed pairwise inner products from the identity matrix.)

A small defect for a small accepted set does not certify that all input
directions were retained. The diagnostic itself uses ordinary double dot
products and can underreport errors through rounding. Values below machine
precision are measured cancellation artifacts, not a certificate of that many
accurate digits. Independent high-precision checks evaluate the stored basis
separately. The plot uses only strictly positive measured defects; a computed
zero is reported in the table and omitted from the logarithmic curve without a
floor.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gram_conditioning.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    std::print("| epsilon | passes | accepted | max Gram defect | projection residual ratio |\n| ---: | " "---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    std::vector<double> x1, y1, x2, y2;
    for (double eps : {1e-2, 1e-4, 1e-6, 1e-8, 1e-10, 1e-12, 1e-13}) {
        const auto v = dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt_near_parallel(eps);
        for (unsigned passes : {1u, 2u}) {
            dm::algorithms::GramSchmidtOptions o;
            o.passes = passes;
            const auto r = dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt_run(v, o);
            const auto quality =
                dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt_quality(v, r.basis);
            const double error =
                quality.get_column<double>("max_gram_error").values()[0], residual = quality.get_column<double>("relative_projection_residual").values()[0];
            std::print("| {:.0e} | {} | {} | {:.12e} | {:.12e} |\n", eps,
                       passes, r.basis.size(), error, residual);
            if (error > 0) {
                (passes == 1 ? x1 : x2).push_back(-std::log10(eps));
                (passes == 1 ? y1 : y2).push_back(std::log10(error));
            }
        }
    }
    std::print("\nScale control: unit axes retain {}; axes scaled by 1e-13 retain {}.\n",
               dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt({{1, 0}, {0, 1}}).size(),
               dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt({{1e-13, 0}, {0, 1e-13}}).size());
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x1, y1, "one pass", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x1, y1, "",
                                                        {37, 99, 235}, 4).line(
        x2, y2, "two passes", {220, 38, 38}, 2).points(x2, y2, "",
                                                       {220, 38, 38}, 4).title(
        "Measured orthogonality: read accepted counts too").x_label(
        "-log10(epsilon)").y_label("log10(max Gram defect)").y_limits(-26, 0).save_svg("build/gram_conditioning.svg");
}
