// result_end
\caption(Each point is a positive native diagnostic. The table exposes threshold-based loss of directions, so an apparent improvement after rejection is not interpreted as improved full-span accuracy.)
\alttext(Blue and red curves compare one and two projection passes as the rows approach parallelism. Two passes reduce measured overlap in the retained set; accepted counts remain essential to interpretation.)

The projection-residual diagnostic is \(\|V-VQ^TQ\|_F/\|V\|_F\) with the
row-vector convention interpreted one input row at a time: subtract
\(\sum_i(v\cdot q_i)q_i\). When Q is not orthonormal this is not the
least-squares projection or a best-span error. Empty bases have a vacuous zero
Gram defect; for nonzero input their projection residual ratio is one.

> Count work and actual retained state

Let \(k\) input rows have width \(d\), and let \(r_j\) be the accepted count
before processing row j. With s passes, the producing projection count and
selected arithmetic are

$$P=s\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}r_j,\quad D=P+k,\quad
T_{dot}=d(P+k),\quad T_{update}=dP,\quad T_{divide}=dr.$$
\alttext(Projection count is the pass count times the sum of prior accepted directions. There is one extra self-dot per input row; each dot or update visits d coordinates, and normalization divides d coordinates for each accepted row.)

If all k rows are accepted, \(P=sk(k-1)/2\), giving \(O(sk^2d)\) arithmetic. Dot
terms count a multiplication and an addition from an initial zero; updates count
a multiplication and subtraction. These counters exclude validation, copying,
allocation, square roots and machine instruction choices. Ordinary
working/output storage is \(O(kd)\).

A full trace owns before and after vectors plus the coefficient for every
projection, and the final residual plus norm for every input row. Its
floating-value units are exactly

$$C=(1+2d)P+(1+d)k.$$
\alttext(Each actual projection retains two d-coordinate residuals and one coefficient, and each row decision retains d residual coordinates and one norm.)

This history can use \(O(k^2d)\) storage. The default one-million-unit cap
admits each complete record before its producing dot and copies; zero disables
the policy cap. It counts floating values, excluding metadata, ordinary output,
vector capacities and byte/peak-memory costs. The separate complete-input cap
counts original rows plus coordinates, including zero-width row headers, and is
checked after full shape/finite/option validation. Neither cap returns truncated
output.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gram_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    std::print("| rows k | passes | projections | dot requests | dot terms | update pairs | divisions | " "captured floats |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    std::vector<double> x, one, two;
    for (std::size_t k : {2, 4, 8, 16, 32}) {
        std::vector<std::vector<double>> v(k, std::vector<double>(k, 0));
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < k; ++i)
            v[i][i] = 1;
        for (unsigned passes : {1u, 2u}) {
            dm::algorithms::GramSchmidtOptions o;
            o.passes = passes;
            const auto t = dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt_trace(v, o);
            const auto &w = t.run.work;
            const auto p =
                passes * k * (k - 1) / 2, expected =
                (1 + 2 * k) * p + (1 + k) * k;
            if (w.projections != p || w.dot_terms != k * (p + k) ||
                w.update_terms != k * p || w.divisions != k * k ||
                t.captured_entries != expected)
                throw std::runtime_error("work identities");
            std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", k, passes,
                       w.projections, w.dot_products, w.dot_terms,
                       w.update_terms, w.divisions, t.captured_entries);
            (passes == 1 ? one : two).push_back(w.projections);
        }
        x.push_back(k);
    }
    bool quota = false, ragged = false, overflow = false;
    dm::algorithms::GramSchmidtOptions o;
    o.max_trace_entries = 10;
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt_trace({{1, 0}, {1, 1}}, o);
    } catch (const std::length_error &) {
        quota = true;
    }
    o.max_trace_entries = 11;
    const auto exact = dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt_trace({{1, 0}, {1, 1}}, o);
    o.max_input_entries = 1;
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt_trace({{1, 0}, {1}}, o);
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument &) {
        ragged = true;
    }
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt({{1e308, 0}});
    } catch (const std::overflow_error &) {
        overflow = true;
    }
    std::print("\nOne-short trace cap rejects: {}; exact cap retains {} floating values; ragged input " "rejected before caps: {}; norm overflow rejected: {}.\n",
               quota, exact.captured_entries, ragged, overflow);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, one, "one pass", {37, 99, 235}, 2).line(x, two, "two passes",
                                                      {220, 38, 38}, 2).title(
        "Actual projection work on independent coordinate axes").x_label(
        "input rows k (dimension d = k)").y_label("projection dot/subtract steps").y_limits(0, 1200).save_svg("build/gram_work.svg");
    if (!quota || !ragged || !overflow)
        throw std::runtime_error("validation controls");
}
