// result_end
\caption(Every count comes from actual kernel work and is checked against a separately derived formula. Zero coefficients still incur the projection dot and update loop; the plot counts these operations.)
\alttext(Two-pass work is twice one-pass projection work for independent coordinate axes. The counts grow quadratically with the number of accepted input rows.)

Malformed row widths, nonfinite coordinates, negative/nonfinite tolerances and
pass counts other than one or two are invalid, even for empty input where
relevant. Nonfinite dots or updates now throw instead of emitting invalid basis
vectors. The original unscaled squared norm can underflow; a tiny nonzero row
can therefore be skipped. The routine intentionally retains this numerical
scheme rather than substituting a scaled norm. Zero-width rectangular rows are
valid and all skipped.

> Orthogonalize real measurement columns

The canonical Iris dataset has 150 observations and four numeric measurements.
Each centered measurement column becomes one vector in \(\mathbb R^{150}\); the
algorithm therefore receives four rows of width 150, not 150 four-dimensional
observations. Centering subtracts each column mean, without variance scaling.
The variable order fixes the first direction and subsequent residuals. Two
passes are selected explicitly for this demonstration.

Species labels color the figure after the basis is computed. They do not enter
centering or orthogonalization. Each plotted point takes its coordinates from
the first two accepted basis rows at that observation. This is an
order-dependent feature representation, not PCA, classification, a train/test
evaluation or a claim of variance-optimal separation. Reordering the measurement
columns can change the picture.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gram_iris.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
    const std::vector<std::string> names{"Sepal.Length", "Sepal.Width",
                                         "Petal.Length", "Petal.Width"};
    const auto columns =
        dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt_columns(iris, names, true);
    dm::algorithms::GramSchmidtOptions o;
    o.passes = 2;
    const auto r = dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt_run(columns, o);
    std::print("Iris observations {}; selected columns {}; vector width {}; passes {}; accepted directions " "{}.\n\n",
               iris.nrows(), columns.size(), r.work.dimension, o.passes,
               r.basis.size());
    dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions format;
    format.scientific = {"max_gram_error", "relative_projection_residual"};
    format.digits = 10;
    std::print("{}\n",
               dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt_quality(columns, r.basis).to_md(
               format));
    std::print("| accepted basis | source measurement | original column index |\n| ---: | --- | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < r.basis.size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", i, names[r.input_rows[i]],
                   r.input_rows[i]);
    std::print("\nActual projections {}; dot requests {}; dot terms {}; update pairs {}; divisions {}.\n",
               r.work.projections, r.work.dot_products, r.work.dot_terms,
               r.work.update_terms, r.work.divisions);
    dm::algorithms::gram_schmidt_scores_plot(r,
                                             iris.get_column<std::string>(
                                             "Species").values()).save_svg(
        "build/gram_iris.svg");
    if (r.basis.size() != 4 || r.work.dimension != 150)
        throw std::runtime_error("Iris feature orientation");
}
