\alttext(A scatter plot displays the first two accepted centered Iris feature directions, with one point per flower and colors for the three species. The axes are normalized feature coordinates, not principal components.)

> What the examples establish

Exact Gram–Schmidt preserves span and produces orthogonal directions. The native
one-pass computation approximates that model under finite precision and an
absolute skip threshold. Actual states reveal how projection subtraction works;
threshold and ill-conditioning controls show why an accepted count or a small
Gram defect alone is insufficient. A second pass improves the measured
retained-set orthogonality in the displayed examples at an explicit work cost.
It does not turn an absolute threshold into a rank certificate.

The numerical discussion follows L. Giraud, J. Langou and M. Rozložník, [The
loss of orthogonality in the Gram–Schmidt orthogonalization
process](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.camwa.2005.08.009) (2005), with an accessible
[primary-paper
copy](https://disciplinas.ime.unicamp.br/ms993/wp-content/uploads/sites/8/2023/08/2005-CMA-giraud-langou-rozloznik.pdf).
Their analyses distinguish conditioning-sensitive single passes from
reorthogonalization under nonsingularity assumptions. The particular absolute
threshold and unscaled norm are this implementation's policy; no general
stability theorem is claimed for all accepted or rejected inputs.

Verification compares ordinary one-pass arithmetic against a frozen
pre-conversion implementation, reconstructs actual trace operations and
counters, and checks malformed, finite-range, threshold, quota and ownership
cases with sanitizers. Independent high-precision oracles check the worked pair,
conditioning and Iris diagnostics. All complete programs, local figures,
documentation examples and both exports are checked before the legacy report is
retired.
